<ul>
  <li><strong>Orchestration and recovery.</strong> A scanner discovers uncollected logs and triggers bounded batches. Access and write-permission checks run before collection; failed logs retry independently, and output manifests report actual stored results. Production runs are triggered on demand; daily scanning is implemented and available.</li>
  <li><strong>Sensor-clock synchronization.</strong> Header-only measurements estimate each log’s trigger offsets and timing jitter. Segmented offsets follow drift during a recording; delayed matching aligns sensor data, CAN, and poses at the frame timestamp rather than at publication or write time.</li>
  <li><strong>Traceable geometric labels.</strong> Each sequence records the calibration rig and its source, clock selection, timing measurements, and quality thresholds. I also recalibrated fleet vehicles and used the revised rigs for recollection or explicit relabeling.</li>
  <li><strong>Select useful views before downloading.</strong> Pose-aware deduplication limits repeated viewpoints and per-session dominance while retaining collected source data in object storage. Calibration labels and train/validation assignments remain traceable through dataset indexes.</li>
</ul>

<p>Timing measurements across <strong>16 fleet logs</strong> also exposed stale camera pairing, incorrect timing gates, and missing-sensor failure paths in the onboard calibration stack. I documented five issues and proposed fixes for the responsible onboard team.</p>
